# Non-relativistic quantum mechanics: first-principles prose scaffold

## Introduction: the entire adopted framework, before its consequences

This sequence adopts ordinary complex-Hilbert-space quantum theory, with a **nonrelativistic fixed-particle-number massive-matter core**, followed by explicitly additional **radiation and light–matter assumptions**. “First principles” means an explicit physical assumption set and complete conditional deductions; it does not mean deriving nature from mathematics. The full set below is complete for the declared core plus its chosen ideal measurement model, not for quantum field theory, gravity, arbitrary apparatuses or all physical systems. Quantum circuits, quantum computing and algorithms are deferred. This is an owner-authorized research design before engine planning: no production items, run readiness, source receipts, publication or independent acceptance are claimed.

**P1 — State and composition postulate.** Assign each system a nonzero separable complex Hilbert space H. Adopt all unit rays in H as pure preparations within a declared sector: `[ψ]={zψ:z∈C\{0}}`, represented by ||ψ||=1. General preparations are positive self-adjoint trace-class operators ρ:H→H with tr ρ=1. The rank-one pure operator is `Rψ u=(u,ψ)ψ`. Statistical mixing with probabilities w_j gives sum_j w_jρ_j in trace norm. Independent distinguishable subsystems have H_A⊗̂H_B, the completed Hilbert tensor product, and local quantities act as A⊗I and I⊗B. Product preparations are a special case; general composite states need not factor. Superselection restrictions, if imposed, are extra model assumptions, not consequences of vector-space linearity. For identical species choose symmetric or antisymmetric tensor sectors as P6 below. The existence of a Hilbert tensor product is mathematics; its assignment to a composite physical system is a physical assumption.

**P2 — Observable and probability postulate.** An ideal sharp real-valued observable is a self-adjoint densely defined operator A:D(A)⊂H→H, with a unit declared for its real spectral coordinate a. Its spectral PVM E_A maps Borel subsets of R to bounded orthogonal projections. The probability of outcome in B is μρ^A(B)=tr(ρE_A(B)); for a unit pure ψ it is ||E_A(B)ψ||². This is a probability measure, not necessarily a density or a discrete sum. Continuous spectrum does not furnish a normalizable eigenvector at every a. An outcome belongs to the spectral support, and “an exact continuous value” is not a finite-resolution detector bin. To describe unsharp finite outcomes additionally allow a POVM F_a≥0, sum F_a=I, with p_a=tr ρF_a. This operational extension requires an apparatus instrument, not just an observable symbol.

**P3 — Ideal conditional measurement postulate.** For a declared ideal repeatable sharp finite-resolution or discrete projective measurement with mutually orthogonal projectors P_a summing to I, outcome a of nonzero probability updates ρ to P_aρP_a/tr(ρP_a); discarding the outcome gives sum_a P_aρP_a. This is the adopted Lüders instrument, preserving coherence inside a degenerate eigenspace. It is not derived from the Born probabilities or asserted for every laboratory detector. General instruments specify their own state update; photon absorption in particular need not leave an outgoing one-photon polarization state. Measurement of an exact continuous eigenvalue is not represented by normalizing a delta distribution. Unitary system–apparatus models explain conditional correlations but do not, alone, establish a unique recorded outcome or solve the interpretation problem.

**P4 — Dynamics postulate.** An isolated autonomous system has a specified self-adjoint energy operator H with units J. Its evolution is U(t)=exp(−itH/ℏ), t in seconds, and ρ(t)=U(t)ρ(0)U(t)*. Every Hilbert vector has continuous evolution; `iℏ dψ/dt=Hψ` is a norm-differentiable equation only for ψ(0)∈D(H). Time-dependent finite-dimensional Hermitian H(t) uses a unitary two-time propagator U(t,s), not the exponential of an integral unless a commutation theorem applies. More general unbounded time-dependent H(t) needs a separate existence/domain theorem. Open-system maps are extensions obtained from an explicitly chosen environment/model, not a replacement secretly built into P4.

**P5 — Nonrelativistic kinematics and massive-particle model postulate.** Adopt Newtonian spacetime M=R×R³ with absolute time t, Euclidean spatial metric on each time slice and an inertial coordinate frame `(t,x)`; inertial frames are related by t'=t+t₀, x'=Rx−vt+a, R∈SO(3). These transformations specify the frame convention, not Lorentzian geometry. A classical preparation/apparatus trajectory is a C¹ curve γ:I→M, γ(t)=(t,x(t)); a quantum particle does not thereby acquire a definite worldline between measurements. For one spin-s particle choose H=L²(R³,d³x)⊗C^(2s+1). The position operators Q_j multiply by x_j, domain {ψ:x_jψ∈L²}; momentum P_j=−iℏ∂_j has its maximal weak-derivative domain {ψ:∂_jψ∈L²}. Derive their self-adjointness through multiplication/Fourier conjugation. They obey [Q_j,P_k]=iℏδ_jk on the explicitly stated Schwartz core, not as unrestricted products on all H. A chosen spinless Hamiltonian is H=sum_j P_j²/(2m)+V(Q), with m>0 and real V in a class whose self-adjointness is proved. This model choice does not follow merely from P1–P4. Boundary conditions specify a different realization and must be stated. Classical external electromagnetic potentials and Pauli magnetic terms require additional coupling assumptions and defined operators.

**P6 — Identical-particle and spin assignments.** Particle labels in an n-fold tensor product are mathematical factor labels. For a specified bosonic species choose Sym^n H; for a specified fermionic species choose Λ^n H. Permutation invariance and the resulting exclusion statement are then conditional results. Spin is a specified unitary SU(2) representation of dimension 2s+1, s∈{0,1/2,1,...}; S_i=ℏJ_i are self-adjoint finite matrices with [S_i,S_j]=iℏε_ijk S_k. The species' mass, charge, spin, statistics and magnetic coupling constants are empirical model parameters/assignments. The relativistic spin–statistics theorem is not a theorem of this nonrelativistic postulate set.

**P7 — Preparation and apparatus assumptions.** A comparison with data requires a declared preparation map, dynamical isolation approximation, detection efficiency/resolution/background model, and sampling assumptions. IID repetitions are an additional statistical model, not a quantum postulate guaranteed by the apparatus. The claim “this experiment realizes ρ,H,E” is a physically tested identification with uncertainty. It does not follow from the mathematical existence of those objects.

**R1–R3 — Radiation extensions, introduced only on the radiation pages.** A fixed propagation-mode one-photon polarization model has a two-dimensional transverse internal space; it is a restricted manifestation of the abstract Born/state framework, not the massive spatial Hamiltonian with m=0. To derive photon number/energy statistics and spontaneous emission, add quantized transverse radiation modes, bosonic Fock space, and a specified light–matter Hamiltonian and detector model. Adopt photon dispersion E=ℏω=ℏc|k| and p=ℏk for the free vacuum model. Adopt an appropriate coupling (for example a controlled nonrelativistic dipole approximation, with its regime) for absorption/emission. A classical prescribed electromagnetic drive yields stimulated transition calculations but does not by itself derive vacuum spontaneous emission. Relativistic matter, antiparticles, pair creation, covariant local field dynamics and full QED are deferred to a named field-theory destination; they are not disguised as solved core prerequisites.

The postulate count depends on how assumptions are packaged. Tong begins with wavefunction/Born/superposition/Schrödinger assumptions; Binney begins with amplitudes; MIT 8.05 begins from previous wave mechanics and develops operator/composition machinery; Teschl formalizes states/observables/dynamics through spectral theory. Our P1–P7 package deliberately distinguishes mathematical constructions from their physical assignments and exposes the extra Lüders and particle-Hamiltonian assumptions. Density/POVM language is an operational extension, not a claim that all four sources use the same enumeration.

## Fundamental quantities, definitions and units

Use SI throughout and keep ℏ explicit. The following quantities are primitive **within this formulation/model**: t∈R (s), spatial coordinates x∈R³ (m), assigned H and measurement/preparation structures, ℏ>0 (J s), species mass m (kg), charge q (C), spin label s (dimensionless), and specified V:R³×I→R (J) or external potentials/couplings. ℏ is h/(2π); SI fixes h numerically, but the physical applicability of the postulate is empirical. m is not derived by the Schrödinger equation; q and g factors are not derived by the Hilbert formalism. c (m s⁻¹) enters the radiation extension and the validity comparison, not Newtonian kinematics. Vacuum constants in the optional external-field model must follow modern SI conventions; no claim that μ₀ has an exact numerical value.

Derived quantities and their types: ψ∈L²(R^d;C^r) is an a.e. equivalence class with position representation units m^(−d/2), ||ψ||² dimensionless; abstract normalized kets and density operators are dimensionless. Probability μ(B) is dimensionless and a position density ψ*ψ has units m^(−d). A momentum wavefunction normalized against d^dp has units (kg m s⁻¹)^(−d/2). Amplitudes for finite outcomes are complex dimensionless scalars; amplitudes depending on detector coordinate have the square-root density units of their specified measure. Energy eigenvalues E and H have J; momentum P has kg m s⁻¹; Q has m; L=Q×P and S have J s. Velocity P/m has m s⁻¹ in the free spinless model; probability current j has m^(1−d) s⁻¹, not charge current. Force −∇V has N in a differentiable external-potential model. Mean, variance and standard deviation have respectively unit u, u² and u of the outcome; a mean exists only with its required moment integrability. Density-matrix eigenvalues, purity tr ρ² and entropy −tr ρ log ρ are dimensionless; physical entropy includes a separately assigned k_B in J K⁻¹. Temperature T (K) and canonical equilibrium ρ∝exp(−H/k_BT) are added ensemble assumptions, not derived from unitary mechanics. Cross section has m² and dσ/dΩ m² sr⁻¹; frequency ν has Hz and angular frequency ω rad s⁻¹ (radian dimensionless), with ω=2πν. State phase is dimensionless and global phase is redundant; relative phase affects probabilities for specified measurement.

Every planned technical concept below uses this dictionary or an exact supplier. Further objects: D(A) is a dense linear subspace, the adjoint has its actual domain, a common core is graph-norm dense, spectrum is the complement of bounded everywhere-invertible resolvent, projection is P²=P=P*, and spectral compatibility means commuting PVMs, not merely a formal zero commutator. A Hamiltonian quadratic form q_H has a declared form domain D(|H|^(1/2)); use it for variational claims rather than assume every trial vector lies in D(H). A wave packet is a normalized L² state with specified localization/moments, not a normalizable plane wave. A stationary state is an energy eigenray; a general superposition of energy eigenvectors has time-dependent relative phase. Degeneracy means dimension of an eigenspace. A bound state is a normalizable eigenstate in the selected spectral regime; an asymptotic scattering state requires defined wave operators. Operators representing a conserved quantity commute in the appropriate spectral/dynamical sense, with domain conditions. Orbital rotations act by `(U(R)ψ)(x)=ψ(R⁻¹x)`; spin rotations act by their declared SU(2) representation; translations act by ψ(x−a), generated by P. Gauge transformations of external potentials and ψ require an explicit coupling theorem. A WKB expression is an asymptotic ansatz with an error regime, not an exact state construction.

