## Deeper development and precise conditional claims

The core should begin with the complete adopted assumptions above, then explain how superposition gives interference while a mixture erases the cross term. An elementary state is not “half a particle”: a unit ray with Born outcomes describes an ensemble of identically specified preparations. For `Ψ=αψ1+βψ2`, normalize by its actual Hilbert norm, including overlap; the zero combination is excluded. A density operator unifies classical preparation uncertainty and subsystem reduction but its decomposition is nonunique. Pure-state entanglement means nonproduct in a declared tensor decomposition; mixed separability is a convex-mixture definition with topology specified in infinite dimension. The Schmidt calculation is finite initially; M10 now supplies the infinite Schmidt norm convergence.

Measurement exposition must distinguish preparation variance, apparatus resolution and disturbance. Repeatability of the declared Lüders measurement follows because a conditional state lies in the selected projector range; it does not prove every real detector is ideal. Observables have domains, so the expression Aψ may be undefined even though ψ is a legitimate state. The general Born law still exists, and moments may fail to exist. Compatible sharp measurements use commuting spectral projections. Formal `[A,B]ψ=0` on a small domain is not sufficient for strong spectral commutation. Finite POVMs/instruments can be developed completely; M32 supplies explicitly measurable continuous Kraus instruments; universal representation remains a separate contract.

Dynamics and kinematics should prove norm/probability conservation through self-adjoint unitary evolution, then derive the local continuity equation only for sufficiently regular ψ and real local V. With ψ:I→H sufficiently differentiable and spatial derivatives justified, `∂t n+∇·j=0`, `n=ψ*ψ`, `j=(ℏ/m)Im(ψ*∇ψ)`; boundary integration needs vanishing flux or explicit boundary conditions. The screen distribution in a two-path arrangement is normalized on the declared detection region and includes weights, loss conditioning and detector response. Orthogonal path records lead to a reduced-state cross term weighted by their overlap; loss of visibility can come from entanglement, phase averaging or apparatus/sampling limitations. None is a theorem that the particle “really took” one definite path.

Exact models come before uncontrolled heuristics: free Gaussian packets; real bounded potentials; finite barrier matching with a current conservation calculation; a separate interval domain for the ideal infinite box; oscillator completeness, not merely ladder algebra; and hydrogen with reduced mass and stated Coulomb idealization. Separate the mathematical eigenproblem from the physical model assignment. With a fixed proton, m is the electron mass; the center-of-mass-separated two-body model uses reduced mass μ=m_e m_p/(m_e+m_p), not a rhetorical correction. The familiar Coulomb energy `E_n=−μq^4/[2(4π ε₀)²ℏ² n²]` is conditional on charges ±q, point Coulomb potential and nonrelativistic spinless Hamiltonian; derive and dimension-check it independently rather than reproduce Tong's erroneous printed formula. Binding levels alone do not derive photons or line intensities.

Symmetry exposition needs actual maps and representations before generators. Orbital angular momentum is self-adjoint generator data for rotations and has a common core where differential commutators are verified. Intrinsic spin is an additional representation assignment, not a tiny rotating charged sphere. Addition of angular momenta occurs in the tensor product and requires a complete finite ladder/Clebsch–Gordan argument. Identical-particle symmetrization precedes exchange energy, Slater determinants and the Pauli exclusion deduction. Entanglement/singlet correlations precede Bell assumptions. Bell deductions exclude a specified setting-independent local-response model when combined with qualified observations, not all hidden-variable theories; quantum no-signalling under discarded local outcomes is a separate conditional theorem.

Approximations must state their controls. The variational bound uses the form domain and spectral lower bound, not necessarily an attained ground state. Stationary perturbation needs an isolated spectral gap and quantitative smallness; degenerate first-order shifts diagonalize the perturbation within the eigenspace. Lifting degeneracy does not force an instantaneous transition to the lowest branch. A closed unitary system's preparation and dynamics determine branch populations; equilibration would require an environmental model. Time-dependent perturbation uses a proved propagator, the interaction picture and a controlled remainder; Fermi's golden rule needs long-time/continuum assumptions and is not a universal exact law. WKB requires smoothness, a semiclassical parameter and turning-point treatment. Adiabatic claims need regularity and a gap, and address evolution of an initially specified spectral subspace, not spontaneous ground-state selection.

Scattering deserves more than barrier tunnelling: define the absolutely continuous subspace, asymptotic free comparison, wave-operator limits and their decay hypotheses, then S and cross sections with flux normalization. Phase shifts and partial waves follow from central short-range models; Coulomb scattering needs a modified long-range comparison. Distinguish bound, resonant and scattering spectra; an outgoing formal wave is not a normalizable eigenstate. Many-body examples can then use physically declared interactions and exchange sectors. A finite canonical ensemble is mathematically defined by a trace-normalized exponential; a general thermodynamic-limit theorem is a separate substantial undertaking, not implied by unitary mechanics alone.

Photon examples appear twice for pedagogical reasons without importing massive-particle kinematics: fixed-mode polarization is an early finite-state manifestation, while quantized radiation is developed after the oscillator and bosonic composition. For an ideal polarization analyzer P_θ onto `(cos θ,sin θ)`, a fixed direction monochromatic photon model gives the transmitted probability cos²θ; a classical wave model can predict the same intensity ratio. Identical ratios alone do not establish field quantization. Photon number statistics, localized detector records and emission require radiation/detection models. A many-photon coherent state, an intensity field and a one-photon state are different mathematical objects. A general photon position wavefunction satisfying the massive Schrödinger equation is not introduced. Boundaries remain explicit: recoil/fine structure/spin–orbit corrections may be future controlled effective models; pair creation and relativistic matter need a relativistic field theory.

## Evidence and classification contracts

The four main theoretical sources are not four experimental reports. Their historical illustrations and photographs motivate the framework but do not supply a complete trial-level uncertainty analysis. No proposed [E] is ready merely because it appears in a textbook. An actual experiment item must cite/read a primary or authoritative full report and record question; source-specific preparation/apparatus; calibration; measured outputs; trial count when reported; finite resolution/loss/background; reported statistical treatment/systematics; and source omissions. Missing precision stays missing. Suitable research destinations are single-electron two-path detection, a single-photon polarization/counting experiment, Stern–Gerlach splitting, atomic spectroscopy and a Bell test; these are candidates, not fabricated records or universal proofs.

For double-slit exposition each record is localized. The ideal coherent model predicts a normalized density proportional to |a1(x)+a2(x)|²; the specified mixture predicts |a1(x)|²+|a2(x)|² after its own normalization. Opening both paths may change the apparatus, so the mixture comparison must state what is held fixed. Recorded-bin probabilities integrate the density and detector response; a pointwise zero at an ideal dark fringe is not a zero finite-bin probability. Finite samples can fail a specified visible-fringe criterion with positive probability under an ordinary nondegenerate coherent sampling model. Do not assign a universal failure probability or treat missing fringes as automatic falsification. Classical waves also interfere; compare specified coherent/mixed/path models instead of “quantum versus all classical physics.” Agreement supports a qualified model and auxiliary assumptions; it does not prove QM. p-values are not probabilities that a theory is false; confidence levels are not posterior theory probabilities.

Definitions [D] introduce objects and operational meanings; [P] adopts assumptions; [M] proves mathematical structures without physical premises; [T] deduces a conditional physical statement; [H] is an idealized setup with the same complete proof duty as [T]; [E] faithfully reports uncertain findings; [R] records limits/context without silently supplying unproved premises. For example, the Gaussian ΔQΔP calculation is [H]/[T], the variance Cauchy–Schwarz inequality is [M], Born interpretation is [P], and reported detections are [E]. P7/experimental identification may enter as `empirical-premise` only when actually used and with uncertainty carried forward. Source/evidence support links use `relations`, never a cyclic proof dependency.

## Source integration and current status

Source reading and mathematical audit are separate obligations. All four final reader reports have now been read and integrated: Tong, Binney–Skinner, Teschl, and the MIT reader’s MIT/Skinner report. Five complete available works were read by those four researchers: three single-volume works and two complete officially posted lecture sets. The posted sets contain disclosed blank/draft sections; complete retrieval/reading is not completeness of mathematical treatment. Skinner’s standalone notes share an author/content with Binney–Skinner and do not count as independent authorship. This scaffold is stable research design, with the open closure status below.

The mathematics audit maps reviewed published interfaces and local arguments M1–M51, with exact completed branches and residual required obligations. The original22 A-page mathematical contracts now have complete local arguments or exact owner-permitted mathematical interfaces, mapped in baseline-claim-coverage.json. This is conditional scaffold closure, not production acceptance or proof that the physical postulates describe nature. Stronger optional/uncommissioned extensions and unreported experiment candidates retain explicit dispositions; they are not used as premises. No engine or root mathematics content was changed.

